--TEST--
An enum case is not an array key, at the subscript that writes one and the subscript that reads it back (`rule:types/arrays`, `rule:enums/closed-integer-type`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:enums/an-enum-as-a-type
// Every key is a `string` and an `int` key normalizes to its own decimal
// string, so an enum case looks like it should key an array by its backing
// value. `rule:enums/closed-integer-type` makes that value a *name*, not a number the program wrote,
// and two enums can carry the same one — so the normalization is refused
// where it is written rather than performed. `$case as int` is the spelling
// for the number, and it keys the array as any `int` does.
enum Mode: int {
    Read = 1,
    Write = 2,
}

array<string> $m = [];
$m[Mode::Read] = "a";
echo $m[Mode::Read], "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0434]: `Mode` is an enum, and an enum case is not an array key
%A
error[E0434]: `Mode` is an enum, and an enum case is not an array key
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
